      <h2>The two-layer structure</h2>
      <p>
        The lead-acid layer is mature and market-anchored — Varta is the region's household brand in replacement
        batteries. The lithium layer is younger and policy-driven: the EU Battery Regulation and automotive demand
        pushed the gigafactory wave, and the same policy now shapes who survives it (see the
        <a href="https://market.worldbatteryhub.com/europe-battery-market/">Europe market report</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>Our Read</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My read:</strong> Europe's manufacturer story is a two-layer structure with a deadline — the
        lead-acid layer is durable, the lithium layer is racing the regulation's compliance clock, and the
        survivors will be those with automotive offtake secured.</p>
        <p><strong>Why:</strong> Demand is not the question; the policy timeline is. The gigafactories live or die on
        reaching scale before the EU's carbon and passport rules penalise imports — a race with a visible clock,
        which makes European manufacturing a policy story as much as an industrial one.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>My editorial view, not investment advice.</em></p>
